      ":question/id": "ea485cdb-41da-5e4e-87b0-f133e79c3191",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-275120",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/1d/1d637a841739052b4e2d26f89bbcfd92eefefcebf6000a46a10a45b7e53ecf15.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b},$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $3\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/33/33a5661bc10830c7e52e2ba7f27779a28c6e5f9cf39630e60d0634f3b6f7ccdd.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3\\mathbf{b}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=3\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{b}+3\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}=3\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3.\n$$"
    },
