      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-327046",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/3d/3d34b42eb1874894af9675944e97e403d67db92bf96449fd8f87fbb14063a10e.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b},$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $\\mathbf{a}.$ So, we begin with the vector $\\mathbf{a},$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/c0/c074e514a4ca52868a5c01d7a29182d62493f2cc955fd0582e52c78bdde2bf7d.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=2|\\mathbf{b}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{b},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda =2\n$$"
    },
    {
      ":db/id": 17592186302312,
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "30a0045e-f1f3-57fa-89c6-bd1617062077",
