      ":question/difficulty": {
        ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
      },
      ":question/id": "49639a9a-7a73-58c8-8e2e-c3ff76e8e2ad",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-120250",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/e1/e1391a86176b1453c05f6ab43f2b5360fac00287e621d8917a142116990688b2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda .$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $3\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/ea/eadc3cc79d5c0f9b58bd8afc6864dca8e5f794cf398921e2ad540b491819562f.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3\\mathbf{b}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{b}+\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1\n$$"
    },
    {
      ":db/id": 17592186420466,
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "271316bb-de9b-5d8b-bad0-ffd1bfc5b101",
              ":answer/type": {
                ":db/ident": ":answer.type/math"
