      ":question/id": "b667d36f-710b-5326-b636-9597ec1ec3f2",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-77230",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/8c/8cf7d0520b1d0654d5749651595acd48b7a08f2b2d55142b18ca63b2ded20f55.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda .$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $2\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/9e/9edea1e3ad727d11d7bfac884f27c1b435233fa7df561dbe2a4f3af45ea16bfa.png)\n\nIn $△OBC$, we have:\n\n- $BC=3|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{BC}=3\\mathbf{a}.$ \n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{b}+3\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=2\\mathbf{b}+\\lambda \\mathbf{a}=2\\mathbf{b}+3\\mathbf{a}.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a}$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$"
    },
    {
      ":db/id": 17592186572666,
