      ":question/difficulty": {
        ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
      },
      ":question/id": "63f90954-aef6-5358-9e26-c4da4dbb21fd",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-327052",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/22/22f309ffc9ff6ae4a43a5faae12e4ed952d65aec70f87a7e71f64594f0c61f92.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{c}=\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b},$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{a}.$ So, we first draw the vector $\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{a}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/b8/b8db5015e22e55282e180b1d81f7a2e1ca544dfe0f468dc4dedb6283bac2da5c.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{a}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{4}|\\mathbf{b}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{c}=\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b}=\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{b},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"
    },
