      ":question/difficulty": {
        ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
      },
      ":question/id": "95085e88-c005-5feb-bf13-36ae5ea5d897",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-70144",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/69/695c825905b268e40b7c77c681c608fcedfed4d0c5150f76224c4e60eb8ab045.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda .$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $2\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/7d/7d388cdd1db5cfe59b447c3fc27b597fc4e42397966935592f56c1c9f4a598e6.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2\\mathbf{b}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{b}+\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1\n$$"
    },
