      ":question/id": "f176b311-90b1-5813-81b9-8b06675237d8",
      ":question/problem": "The arrows show $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf a$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf b$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf c$ on a square grid.\n\n![](images/9c/9c8e6ce5ab61cd32d5173d7dc35c715e54ba53fe5113573bedfe37cc4f2d7bcc.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf c=\\mathbf a+\\lambda\\mathbf b$, find $\\lambda$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "One component is $\\mathbf a$, so begin with $\\overrightarrow{OA}$. The remaining vector is $\\overrightarrow{AC}$.\n\n![](images/a1/a13ee372cbfbea706174ae57c0c78e02ae19d4e469300b23d4ee779f6b815a3e.png)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf b$ goes up two squares. From $A$ to $C$ we go up six squares, in the same direction. Hence\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AC}=\\frac{6}{2}\\mathbf b=3\\mathbf b.\n$$\n\nBy the triangle law,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf c=\\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AC}=\\mathbf a+3\\mathbf b.\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$ are not parallel, so their coefficients are unique. Matching the coefficient of $\\mathbf b$ gives $\\lambda=3$."
    },
    {
      ":db/id": 17592186735386,
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/choices": [
