      ":question/difficulty": {
        ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/hard"
      },
      ":question/id": "43bbfbc7-891c-5b1f-9e1b-23a6a2b10a00",
      ":question/problem": "The arrows show $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf a$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf b$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf c$. Solid grid lines are one unit apart; dotted lines divide each square in half.\n\n![](images/38/3876004b0e8b71376f6da2ec1857555cf931c29be5a83dec6b2934ce17f583d1.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Construct the known component $\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}$ from $O$, and call its endpoint $P$. Count signed horizontal and vertical grid displacements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OP} &=\\frac{1}{2}\\,(-2, 1)=\\left(-1, \\frac{1}{2}\\right). \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{PC} &=\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{2}\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/e2/e2b956ddda0fb60b0ed908451578575f33dce603d78fabd26b1d2ca3dd8f5190.png)\n\nCompare the remaining displacement with $\\mathbf{a}$, whose grid displacement is $(-2, 2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Horizontal scale} &=\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}}{-2}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n\\text{Vertical scale} &=\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}}{2}=\\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scale is positive, so the remaining vector has the same direction as $\\mathbf{a}$. Thus $\\overrightarrow{PC}=\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{a}$. By the triangle law,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c} &=\\overrightarrow{OP}+\\overrightarrow{PC} \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}+\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{a} \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, so their coefficients are unique. Matching the coefficient of $\\mathbf{a}$ gives $\\lambda=\\frac{1}{4}$."
    },
