      ":question/id": "179bf212-0bfb-539a-ad37-65e12d98e79f",
      ":question/problem": "The arrows show $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf a$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf b$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf c$. Grid squares have side length one unit.\n\n![](images/45/450bbc1c472dd6a5f2189bbc1954beb04aa29a2520947ec1a4e3c14a6133103c.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Construct the known component $\\mathbf{a}$ from $O$, ending at $A$. Count signed horizontal and vertical grid displacements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OA} &=1\\,(0, -5)=(0, -5). \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{AC} &=(10, 0).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/c5/c51cfbbc29a6d168dbc17af3246d3a21ff7d9933884d0fe244e2823f6501f555.png)\n\nCompare the remaining displacement with $\\mathbf{b}$, whose grid displacement is $(4, 0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Horizontal scale} &=\\frac{10}{4}=\\frac{5}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scale is positive, so the remaining vector has the same direction as $\\mathbf{b}$. Thus $\\overrightarrow{AC}=\\frac{5}{2}\\mathbf{b}$. By the triangle law,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c} &=\\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AC} \\\\\n&=\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{5}{2}\\mathbf{b} \\\\\n&=\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{5}{2}\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, so their coefficients are unique. Matching the coefficient of $\\mathbf{b}$ gives $\\lambda=\\frac{5}{2}$."
    },
