      ":question/id": "430ba402-fadf-54f3-838b-fc04d5c73c40",
      ":question/problem": "The arrows show $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf a$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf b$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf c$. Grid squares have side length one unit.\n\n![](images/0f/0f345644213f3b78013d06f25e39df9b74ab93a0560ac7611f4b51186c47b905.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Construct the known component $\\mathbf{b}$ from $O$, ending at $B$. Count signed horizontal and vertical grid displacements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OB} &=1\\,(0, -3)=(0, -3). \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{BC} &=(-6, 0).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/8a/8a4ecc321f6a818275a9281cc80a82506b9b1a3f6826cbc6dbd011b798895041.png)\n\nCompare the remaining displacement with $\\mathbf{a}$, whose grid displacement is $(-2, 0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Horizontal scale} &=\\frac{-6}{-2}=3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scale is positive, so the remaining vector has the same direction as $\\mathbf{a}$. Thus $\\overrightarrow{BC}=3\\mathbf{a}$. By the triangle law,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c} &=\\overrightarrow{OB}+\\overrightarrow{BC} \\\\\n&=\\mathbf{b}+3\\mathbf{a} \\\\\n&=3\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, so their coefficients are unique. Matching the coefficient of $\\mathbf{a}$ gives $\\lambda=3$."
