      ":question/id": "a4ca5188-16aa-55da-a359-95d4c2a72918",
      ":question/problem": "The arrows show $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf a$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf b$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf c$. Grid squares have side length one unit.\n\n![](images/03/037a78174621485781dff6e86085e2cf8389ff9ae4e148c37346cb23db911b64.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=3\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Construct the known component $3\\mathbf{a}$ from $O$, and call its endpoint $P$. Count signed horizontal and vertical grid displacements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OP} &=3\\,(1, -1)=(3, -3). \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{PC} &=(-1, 0).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/9a/9a193e20dd46765efa19a097c486108c52549134abe3c55757196ed9045bbcd7.png)\n\nCompare the remaining displacement with $\\mathbf{b}$, whose grid displacement is $(-2, 0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Horizontal scale} &=\\frac{-1}{-2}=\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scale is positive, so the remaining vector has the same direction as $\\mathbf{b}$. Thus $\\overrightarrow{PC}=\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}$. By the triangle law,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c} &=\\overrightarrow{OP}+\\overrightarrow{PC} \\\\\n&=3\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b} \\\\\n&=3\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, so their coefficients are unique. Matching the coefficient of $\\mathbf{b}$ gives $\\lambda=\\frac{1}{2}$."
    },
