      ":question/id": "d479cc32-0b20-5773-bb01-d92fe3db0f2e",
      ":question/problem": "The arrows show $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf a$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf b$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf c$. Grid squares have side length one unit.\n\n![](images/9c/9c588029ebd0ca04c3317cff5891ac0c548d5606eb83bf918fa85ecab57cff00.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Construct the known component $3\\mathbf{b}$ from $O$, and call its endpoint $P$. Count signed horizontal and vertical grid displacements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OP} &=3\\,(-2, 2)=(-6, 6). \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{PC} &=(-2, 0).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/be/bec67a793df45efe9f3a6907514c2cc2d17ed6f4446c87958eb04910787ff4fd.png)\n\nCompare the remaining displacement with $\\mathbf{a}$, whose grid displacement is $(-2, 0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Horizontal scale} &=\\frac{-2}{-2}=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scale is positive, so the remaining vector has the same direction as $\\mathbf{a}$. Thus $\\overrightarrow{PC}=\\mathbf{a}$. By the triangle law,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c} &=\\overrightarrow{OP}+\\overrightarrow{PC} \\\\\n&=3\\mathbf{b}+\\mathbf{a} \\\\\n&=\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, so their coefficients are unique. Matching the coefficient of $\\mathbf{a}$ gives $\\lambda=1$."
    },
    {
