      ":question/difficulty": {
        ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/hard"
      },
      ":question/id": "ee44a393-26e5-5a8c-a2ca-add9fd4d552c",
      ":question/problem": "The arrows show $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf a$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf b$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf c$. Solid grid lines are one unit apart; dotted lines divide each square in half.\n\n![](images/5d/5de299a8b3d58c9a0b5272e9f5ed9e8af52fc47d5e6f5167c3864d437ec9a5ea.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Construct the known component $\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{b}$ from $O$, and call its endpoint $P$. Count signed horizontal and vertical grid displacements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OP} &=\\frac{3}{2}\\,(-2, 1)=\\left(-3, \\frac{3}{2}\\right). \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{PC} &=(-1, 1).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/b1/b1a2796e7ce9f104c863ed52bf7d79c577ecfc02cabfa624401154f35a8aa1db.png)\n\nCompare the remaining displacement with $\\mathbf{a}$, whose grid displacement is $(-2, 2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Horizontal scale} &=\\frac{-1}{-2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n\\text{Vertical scale} &=\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scale is positive, so the remaining vector has the same direction as $\\mathbf{a}$. Thus $\\overrightarrow{PC}=\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}$. By the triangle law,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c} &=\\overrightarrow{OP}+\\overrightarrow{PC} \\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{b}+\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a} \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, so their coefficients are unique. Matching the coefficient of $\\mathbf{a}$ gives $\\lambda=\\frac{1}{2}$."
    }
  ],
  "canonical_example": {
    ":db/id": 17592186096495,
    ":question/id": "839ed5f5-165f-5de5-aba1-551ac605475f",
    ":question/math-academy-id": "e-5212",
    ":question/problem": "Consider the picture below.\n\n![](images/f2/f2518c2e998e7c7f551548a4cc7283720a66428ea8fa9872494a81cc3848ba9e.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda.$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Recall that when two known vectors are not parallel, a linear combination of them is unique. So, once we rewrite $\\mathbf c$ in terms of $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b,$ we can match coefficients to determine the unknown scalar.\n\nTo find $\\lambda,$ we want to match the expression\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2 \\mathbf{b} .$ So, we first draw the vector $2 \\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/ea/ea517ac4ca8e0bcf7ba48f23e66f100323d5485f1221219aacfbea9148f4a1af.png)\n\nNote that in $△ O D C$, we have:\n\n- $D C = 2 | \\mathbf{a} |$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{D C}$ and $\\mathbf a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{D C} = 2 \\mathbf{a} .$\n\nAdditionally,\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\mathbf{c} &  = \\overrightarrow{O D} + \\overrightarrow{D C} \\\\  &  = 2 \\mathbf{b} + 2 \\mathbf{a} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} = 2 \\mathbf{b} + 2 \\mathbf{a} .$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a} \\nparallel \\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf a,$ we get:\n$$\n\\lambda=2\n$$"
  },
